<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-astronauts">
  <title>Who went up in space for how long?</title>

  <introduction>
    <p><q>The sky's the limit.</q> (Idiom)</p>
    <p><em>Background.</em> Human space travel has captured the world's attention since the first spaceflight by Yuri Gagarin in April 1961.</p>
    <p><em>Questions.</em> How many men and women have gone up in space? How long were they in space? Which countries did they come from?</p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> @tidyt:2020b</p>
    <p><em>Structure.</em> 1277 records each of 24 variables.</p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="secAstro1">
    <title>What human spaceflights have there been?</title>

    <p>The Tidytuesday project includes a collection of interesting datasets, adding a new one each week (@tidyt:2020a). The one uploaded in July 2020 (@tidyt:2020b) contained information on manned spaceflights up to the end of 2019. Each flight by each person is a separate record, making 1277 in all. Several flew more than once and there are 565 different people in the dataset. Two Americans, Jerry Ross and Franklin Chiang-Diaz, each flew seven times. There is demographic information on the individuals (sex, year of birth, nationality) and some information on the missions (title, year, spacecraft used, length of mission).</p>

    <p>Human spaceflight may be defined as a human reaching outer space by going beyond 100 km from the earth. The Challenger mission of 1986 that ended in disaster did not get that far, but is included in the dataset. The non-fatal Soyuz mission of September 1983 when the launch vehicle caught fire before launching is not included.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-asty">
      <caption>Numbers of people by year of flight.</caption>
      <image source="images/asty-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The number of people each year is shown in Figure <xref ref="fig-asty"/>. There are sharp drops in numbers after the Soyuz 1 crash in 1967, after the Challenger disaster in 1986 and after the Columbia disaster in 2003. The deaths in Soyuz 11 in 1971 do not seem to have had such an effect, but may have curtailed existing plans of the time.</p>

    <p>Early on, the participants were male and younger than later on. There have been fewer females and they were on average younger than the males. Figure <xref ref="fig-astg"/> is a scatterplot of age by year of mission with smooths plotted separately for males and females. Age has been calculated as the difference between year of mission and year of birth, so is not exact. The vertical scale is extended because of John Glenn's third spaceflight at the age of 77 in 1998.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-astg">
      <caption>Scatterplot of age by year of flight coloured by sex, with smooths and their confidence intervals.</caption>
      <image source="images/astg-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Scatterplots with many points do not convey actual numbers in groups well. Figure <xref ref="fig-astb"/> shows barcharts for males and females across the decades. There have been far fewer females and, in particular, the bar for the only female in the 1960s is barely visible. More went into space in the 1990s than in any other decade.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-astb">
      <caption>Numbers of participants by decade and by sex.</caption>
      <image source="images/astb-1.png"/>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secAstro1a">
    <title>Mission times in space</title>

    <p>One important piece of information is the length of each mission, the time from launch until return to earth. Figure <xref ref="fig-flightt"/> shows a scatterplot of this time against flight year. Most flights were relatively short, especially in the 1960s and later on there were still many short flights of up to a few weeks, but also a group of longer ones, closer to six months, and a few very long ones of up to a year or more.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flightt">
      <caption>Scatterplot of mission time in hours by year of flight.</caption>
      <image source="images/flightt-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>There is some information that might explain why flights are either short or longer, in particular whether the mission involved a visit to a space station. That is the reason as can be seen in Figure <xref ref="fig-flighttc"/>. All the long flights are part of space station missions. The flight in 1980 marked as none was in fact a space station flight by the Cuban Arnaldo Mendez. This is a curious error as his mission time was also incorrectly reported, multiplied by 10 in the dataset.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flighttc">
      <caption>Mission time in hours by year of flight coloured by space station programme.</caption>
      <image source="images/flighttc-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>The code for this plot converted two values of the variable in_orbit to Salyut, those with Salyut in them and those with the misspelling Saluyt. Minor typos like this can be irritating or seriously misleading. The problem was discovered when colouring the display.</p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secAstro2">
    <title>Logging mission times</title>

    <p>As there are so many short mission times that cannot be distinguished, logarithms of the times could be used. Figure <xref ref="fig-flighttl"/> shows the result with an interpretable vertical scale.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flighttl">
      <caption>Scatterplot of log mission time by year of flight coloured by space station programme.</caption>
      <image source="images/flighttl-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>There are several striking features. The two groups of short and long missions look less variable and the number of shorter missions declines after 2010. There are a few unexpected half points at the foot of the plot. These are mission times recorded as zero and hence logarithms of minus infinity. The 1986 value is from the Challenger disaster and the 2018 and 2019 values are for the two on an aborted 2018 mission.</p>

    <p>There are some low outliers. Four of the seven people who died in the Challenger disaster were given times of 0 and the other three times of about 35 minutes. The Space Shuttle Columbia disintegrated on re-entering the atmosphere in early 2003; the dataset records in_orbit as Exploded for only three of the seven. Another lowish value in 1986 was for Bill Nelson. His Wikipedia page (@wikiBN:2020) said he was in space for 6d 02h 03m, so the dataset value likely reflects an error from webscraping.</p>

    <p>Nationality is another factor to consider. Forty nationalities are included in all, but U.S. and U.S.S.R./Russia account for most participants, so a new variable with U.S., U.S.S.R./Russia, and Other was used. Figure <xref ref="fig-flightn"/> uses faceting with ghostplotting. Russians were first with longer flights, while Americans took part in many more shorter flights in the 1980s and 1990s. Other nationalities flew for the first time at the end of the 1970s and had more short flights.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-flightn">
      <caption>Scatterplots of log mission time by year of flight by nationality.</caption>
      <image source="images/flightn-1.png"/>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="secAstro4">
    <title>Checking the data with graphics</title>

    <p>Checking whether other missions had differing times for their crews uncovered a number of inconsistencies. Grouping by mission_title, ascend_shuttle, in_orbit, descend_shuttle, and year_of_mission grouped many correctly, but failed to group all missions properly as different codings and errors were present. A boxplot of the biggest differences in mission times for the groups found is displayed in Figure <xref ref="fig-difs"/>.</p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-difs">
      <caption>Boxplot of biggest differences in times for (possibly) the same missions.</caption>
      <image source="images/difs-1.png"/>
    </figure>

    <p>Ideally, all typos and errors should be corrected. The very first line of the dataset already raises concern. It would be informative to use exact flight dates, not just years, and dates are available on the Wikipedia page for human spaceflights (@wikiSp:2020). The dataset illustrates issues that can arise with publicly available data and how graphics can help identify and deal with them. Even with individual data problems, the dataset still reveals much interesting information.</p>

    <p><em>Answers.</em> Many more men than women have gone up in space and the men have been older. Initial flights were short. Since the space station programmes began, people have stayed much longer in space. Most have been Russians or Americans, but this is changing.</p>
    <p><em>Further questions.</em> How many spaceflights have individuals made? Are there differences between U.S.S.R./Russians and Americans?</p>

    <p><em>Graphical takeaways.</em></p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>Graphics uncover problems in datasets, be they typos, errors or more serious issues. (Sections <xref ref="secAstro1a"/> and <xref ref="secAstro4"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Scatterplots are excellent, but it is well to check for duplicate pairs of values and draw additional complementary plots. (Figure <xref ref="fig-astg"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Colours should be used consistently for the same categories; different palettes should be used for other groupings to avoid confusion. (Figures <xref ref="fig-astg"/> and <xref ref="fig-astb"/> versus <xref ref="fig-flighttc"/> or <xref ref="fig-flightn"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Log transformations are effective for skewed distributions, especially with interpretable scaling. (Figure <xref ref="fig-flighttl"/>)</p></li>
      <li><p>Faceting disentangles overlapping groups in scatterplots. (Figure <xref ref="fig-flightn"/>)</p></li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</chapter>
